Now check if subarray must be contiguous (yes, subarray is contiguous by definition).

Our run approach considers only contiguous runs of same remainder, which is correct since resilient subarray must have all elements same remainder (from ai ≡ S mod k for all i), so it must lie within one run.

Is it possible to have a resilient subarray with elements from two different remainder runs? No, because if a subarray contains two elements with different remainders mod k, say a[i] ≡ r1, a[j] ≡ r2, r1 ≠ r2, then S ≡ a[i] ≡ r1 and S ≡ a[j] ≡ r2 mod k, so r1 ≡ r2 mod k, contradiction. Thus all elements in a resilient subarray must have same remainder mod k, hence must be within one run.

